{"summary":"The bill would require each school district and public charter school to set aside one instructional day each school year to observe the Fourth of July through age-appropriate instruction for students in kindergarten through grade 12. The instruction would cover the founding of the United States, the original colonies’ separation from Great Britain, the Declaration of Independence, the Constitution, and fundamental rights and freedoms.

Schools could choose their instructional programs and methods, but the lessons would have to meet grade-specific goals. Students in kindergarten through grade 5 would learn basic ideas of independence, freedom, rights, and national symbols. Students in grades 6 through 8 would study the events and reasons behind independence, the founding documents, and civic principles. Students in grades 9 through 12 would analyze the founding documents and themes of the American Revolution and connect founding principles to citizenship today.

School district and charter school directors would have to designate the day annually, ensure the instruction is carried out, verify that it meets the bill’s requirements, and provide guidance as needed. Instruction given in a social studies or English language arts class would also have to align with relevant state academic standards. All instruction would have to be nonpartisan and solely educational. Districts and charter schools would periodically review their materials and methods and update them as they consider appropriate. The bill would take effect upon becoming law and apply beginning with the 2026–2027 school year."}
